    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm looking at some testing data for a new high-performance braking setup. Can you compare the thermal dissipation rates and heat soak characteristics of ceramic-matrix composite (CMC) pads versus standard semi-metallic pads during a high-speed deceleration event? I'm specifically interested in how the thermal gradient affects the rotor surface during repeated heavy braking cycles.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Ceramic-matrix composite (CMC) pads generally have slower thermal dissipation rates compared to standard semi-metallic pads. This slower rate can lead to increased temperatures on the rotor surface, resulting in a higher thermal gradient during repeated heavy braking cycles. Additionally, CMC pads are more prone to heat soak, where the rotor surface temperature remains elevated for longer periods after braking stops, which can compromise braking performance and reduce pad lifespan. In contrast, semi-metallic pads tend to dissipate heat more quickly, but may also experience heat build-up due to less efficient heat dissipation in the pad.",
      "source": "target",
