      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Those sudden, mysterious spikes could be related to micro-erosion or micro-cracking in the rotor material, triggered by repeated thermal cycling and material fatigue. Microscopic defects in the rotor surface, created by wear particles from the brake pads or material imperfections, could act as tiny nucleation sites for localized temperature hotspots. Alternatively, it's also possible that the friction interface itself is experiencing some kind of micro-thermal spiking, potentially caused by changes in the molecular layering or defect populations at the oxide-metal interface.",
